    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is structurally clean, additive, and idiomatic for this codebase.\n\n- **Field + accessors on `Owner`** sit alongside the other persisted fields with the same accessor style and a thorough Javadoc that cross-references the behavioral contract (`CheckHouseholdUnique`). Natural placement.\n- **`DeleteOwner`** keeps its role and name (`Delete<E>` per the naming convention) \u2014 it still \"deletes\" the owner, now by flagging and saving. The Javadoc was updated to match. The soft-delete logic lives in exactly the right unit; nothing leaks elsewhere.\n- **`CheckHouseholdUnique`** gains a one-line `isDeleted()` skip inside its existing `findAll()` loop. This is consistent with how sibling functions in the same package filter inline (e.g. `CountNamesakes` filters by name within its own `findAll()` loop). There is no existing \"active owners\" repository abstraction to reuse, and introducing one (e.g. `findAllActive()`) for a single call site would be premature given the established per-function inline-filter pattern \u2014 the current approach is the idiomatic fit.\n- **Schema changes** are applied uniformly across all four dialects (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres) with a consistent `NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE`, matching the `deleted = false` default for new rows.\n- **`openapi.yml`** documents the field as `readOnly` with a description mirroring the model Javadoc.\n\nNo duplication is introduced, no class is turning into a catch-all, and the responsibilities stay where a maintainer of this pipeline-style codebase would expect them.\n\n(Note on scope: whether other owner-iterating functions such as `CountNamesakes` should also ignore deleted owners is a spec-coverage/correctness question, which this review is explicitly instructed not to judge. Structurally, the code placement is correct.)",
